 "notes": [
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Bones are by eye, the way you do it: chicken to about half the pot, 1–2 pork knees, beef about the same as the chicken or a bit more.</li><li>Dashi volume, marinade and per-bowl amounts are starting points (one whole octopus tentacle per bowl is assumed; the octopus sous vide at <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"temp\" data-u=\"C\" data-a=\"77\" data-s=\"0\">77 °C</span> for ~5 h is assumed too — confirm).</li><li>Adjust to what you did.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Amounts"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Inspired by Momofuku’s ramen (David Chang): like theirs, the broth starts from kombu–shiitake dashi — but no bacon, and the bones go in three separate 6–8 h stages (chicken + onion, pork + ginger + garlic, beef).</li><li>The chashu oven (very hot to colour, then low until tender) follows their pork belly, which is a flat slab; a rolled belly is thicker, so the low stage may need longer — temperatures and times are assumed, confirm.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Momofuku"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>If the broth stays thin and see-through, the boil wasn’t hard enough — crank it and reduce.</li><li>Knuckle/trotter is where most of the body comes from.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Clear vs creamy"
  },
  {
   "html": "<p>The <a href=\"ramen-egg.html\">6-minute egg</a> goes into water that’s already boiling — not <a href=\"boiled-eggs.html\">Dawn’s boiled eggs</a>, which start cold and finish off the heat.</p>",
   "title_html": "The egg"
  },
  {
   "html": "<p>In the fridge, chashu, tare and octopus keep 4–5 days; for longer, see <i>Make ahead &amp; store</i>.</p>",
   "title_html": "Keeping"
  }
 ],
